Goal
Fix a bug in FastAPI's APIRouter that prevents using an empty path ("") in a 
path operation when the router has no prefix, by correcting path normalization 
logic in the routing layer.

Files to modify
  • fastapi/routing.py
  • tests/test_api_router.py

Implementation steps
  1. Step 1: In fastapi/routing.py, modify the APIRouter.add_api_route method to
remove or relax any validation or string concatenation logic that rejects or 
corrupts an empty path string when self.prefix is empty or None. Ensure the path
is normalized to a valid route string (e.g., resolving to '/') before passing it
downstream.
  2. Step 2: In fastapi/routing.py, apply the same path handling correction to 
APIRouter.add_api_websocket_route so WebSocket routes also support empty paths 
in prefixless routers.
  3. Step 3: In tests/test_api_router.py, add a test case that creates an 
APIRouter without a prefix, defines an operation using an empty path (e.g., 
router.get('')), and asserts that the generated route path is '/' and that 
client requests to '/' return the expected response.
  4. Step 4: Run tests to confirm the new test fails on the main branch and 
passes after the fix, ensuring 100% coverage is maintained and no regressions 
occur in prefixed router behavior.

Tests to run
  $ pytest tests/test_api_router.py -v

Risks
  ⚠  Changing path normalization may inadvertently affect how prefixed routers 
concatenate empty paths, so prefixed router tests must pass unchanged.
  ⚠  Starlette expects paths to start with '/', so the empty path must be 
normalized to '/' before route registration to avoid runtime routing errors.

